# Introduction and package description

Multilevel models (also known as hierarchical linear models, linear mixed-effect models,
mixed models, nested data models, random coefficient, random-effects models, random
parameter models, or split-plot designs) are statistical models of parameters that vary at
more than one level. An example could be a model of student performance that contains
measures for individual students as well as measures for classrooms within which the
students are grouped. These models can be seen as generalizations of linear models (in
particular, linear regression), although they can also extend to non-linear models.

Metida.jl is a Julia package for fitting mixed-effects models with flexible covariance
structure.

See documentation:

  * Dev: [https://pharmcat.github.io/Metida.jl/dev/](https://pharmcat.github.io/Metida.jl/dev/)
  * Stable: [https://pharmcat.github.io/Metida.jl/stable/](https://pharmcat.github.io/Metida.jl/stable/)

## Validation purpose

The main validation purpose is confirmation by examination and provision of objective
evidence that software specifications conform to user needs and intended uses, and that the
particular requirements implemented through software can be consistently fulfilled.

Fitting a mixed model is a complex task: initial conditions, the random- and repeated-effect
model and the structure of the data all affect the result. Metida.jl offers many
combinations of settings and it is not possible to check them all. Within the scope of this
validation every public method is exercised at least once, and for the critical tasks wide
data examples are analysed.

## Acceptance criteria

### Kinds of evidence

Checks in this report rest on four kinds of claim, and they are **not interchangeable**. The
chapter *Operation qualification* reports, for every section of the test suite, how many of
each it makes.

  * **External** - the corresponding value from an independent implementation: IBM SPSS
    Statistics 28, MixedModels.jl fitted in the same run, or a published table. This is the
    evidence of the *Performance qualification* chapter.
  * **Relation** - an identity between two quantities computed in the same run, such as
    `aic = -2logml + 2p`, the ordering of two confidence intervals, or two code paths that
    must reach the same number. No constant is stored, so nothing can go stale, and the
    claim holds whatever the data.
  * **Property** - a claim with nothing to compare against: positive definiteness, symmetry,
    a rank, a row count, a flag for a non-estimable contrast.
  * **Internal** - a value this package produced and that is carried forward unchanged. It
    establishes that the behaviour has not changed and **nothing more**; it is not evidence
    of correctness. The reference dictionary marks every such value with
    `source = metida`, and the tables below report how many there are.

Behaviour of a call - that it completes quietly, warns with a given text, or throws a given
exception type - is recorded in the same journal and counted separately.

### The criterion

A single comparison is accepted when

$|x_{Metida} - x_{ref}| \le r_{tol} \times |x_{ref}| + a_{tol}$

The tolerance is **not one number for the whole report**, and that is deliberate. Each
quantity carries its own, from the same place as its reference:

  * in the dictionary the tolerance sits in the `atol` and `rtol` columns next to the value,
    so the test source contains neither the number nor the tolerance;
  * the validation categories state theirs in their own headers: `1e-6` to the SPSS value
    for `-2logREML`, `1e-4` to MixedModels.jl, half of the last printed digit for the
    published bioequivalence table;
  * where a claim is a relation or a property there is no tolerance to state - the identity
    either holds to machine precision or the claim is false.

A single tolerance over all of it would either demand more than a reference printed to six
significant digits can support, or allow less than a comparison with a closed form should.
Where a scenario states no tolerance the defaults $r_{tol}$ = `j ACCEPT_RTOL` and $a_{tol}$
= `j ACCEPT_ATOL` apply.

The report is accepted only when **all** of the following hold:

  * every record of the API check journal passed;
  * every record the report made itself passed, which includes the package test suite and
    the three validation categories;
  * every required scenario reported its results.

The third condition is not redundant: the document generator renders a failing code chunk
into the document and carries on, which would otherwise drop a results table without any
other sign. The same applies to the Conclusion, which marks itself once it has rendered.

### Where the verdict is

This report is produced whatever the outcome of the checks, and it carries its own verdict.
The verdict draws on three sources and none of them substitutes for another:

| Source | What it holds | Written by |
|---|---|---|
| `test/references/testlog.csv` | one row per check of the API test suite | the test suite itself |
| the acceptance log | the package test run and the three validation categories | this report |
| the list of required scenarios | that every chapter reported at all | this report |

The Conclusion states the verdict, summarises every scenario and lists **every divergence**.
The acceptance log is written next to this report as `docs/src/validation_acceptance.tsv`
and the divergences as `docs/src/validation_divergences.tsv`, so they can be read without
opening the PDF. An empty divergence file is itself a statement: it is written even when
nothing diverged.

Independently of the report, `acceptance_verdict()` in `test/acceptance.jl` is called
outside the document generator, after the report is built, and aborts the documentation
build on a negative verdict - the generator swallows errors raised inside a code chunk, so a
verdict computed only inside the report could never fail.

## Requirements

  * Julia 1.8 (or higher) installed for an Operating System / OS Version / Architecture in
    the Tier 1 list
  * Julia packages from the dependency list installed (see
    [Project.toml](https://github.com/PharmCat/Metida.jl/blob/master/Project.toml))

*Tier 1: Julia is guaranteed to build from source and pass all tests on these platforms when
built with the default options. Official binaries are always available and CI is run on every
commit to ensure support is actively maintained.*

## Developer software life cycle

  * Development stage
  * Testing procedures development
  * Performing testing procedures on local machine
  * Push to development branch
  * Make pull request to master branch
  * Performing testing procedures with GitHub Actions
  * Make pull request to the official registry of general Julia packages (if necessary)
  * Make release (if previous completed)

### Versions

  * X.Y.Z - patch release (no breaking changes)
  * X.Y.0 - minor release (may include breaking changes if X = 0)
  * X.0.0 - major release (breaking changes, changes in public API)
  * 0.#.# - no stable public API
  * 1.#.# or higher - stable public API

## Build support

### Tier 1

  * julia-version: 1.8, 1
  * julia-arch: x64
  * os: ubuntu-latest, macOS-latest, windows-latest

