# Operation qualification

This part of validation entails running the software under known conditions with defined
inputs and documented outcomes that can be compared to their predefined expectations. All
documented public API is included in the testing procedures, together with part of the
critical internal methods. Testing procedures can be found in the `test` directory; their
registry is `test/REGISTRY.md`.

## Coverage

Code coverage report available on
[Codecov.io](https://app.codecov.io/gh/PharmCat/Metida.jl).

* Coverage goal: ≥ 90.0%

## Data

For the operation checks generated data is used; for any purpose this data is available in
the repository and included in the package. Model specifications are not written inline in
the tests either: they are recorded in `test/references/models.csv` and
`test/references/effects.csv`, from which the SPSS syntax of the validation runs is also
built.

```julia; echo = false, results = "tex"
print(acceptance_md(dataset_table()))
```
Table: Datasets and the number of model specifications on each

```julia; echo = false, results = "tex"
print(acceptance_md(structure_table()))
```
Table: Covariance structures over the specified effects

## Testing results

The outcome is recorded and enforced: a failing test suite makes `acceptance_verdict()`
abort the build (see *Acceptance criteria*). The `try`/`catch` exists only because the
document generator does not propagate errors raised inside a chunk.

This run comes first in the chapter on purpose: the suite writes the check journal that
every table below reads, so the tables describe the run just made and not a journal left
over from an earlier one.

```julia; echo = true; wrap = false
test_passed = try
    Pkg.test("Metida")
    true
catch err
    @error "Metida test suite failed" exception = (err, catch_backtrace())
    false
end
acceptance_mark("PKG-TEST", test_passed, "Pkg.test(Metida)")
```

## The check journal

No test holds its expected numbers. They live in `test/references/values.csv`, one row per
(key, index), and a check refers to one by key:

```julia; eval = false
@refcheck "api_refmodel.nobs" Metida.nobs(lmm)
```

The macro takes the expected value and the tolerance from the dictionary, compares, expands
into an ordinary `@test` - the run sees it like any other - and appends a row to the
journal. When the run ends the journal holds one row per check: the section it stands in,
the function, the key, the expected and the obtained value, the difference, the tolerance,
the source of the reference, the kind of claim and the outcome. It is written to
`test/references/testlog.csv` whatever the outcome, so an empty failure list is a statement
rather than an absence of data.

```julia; echo = false, results = "tex"
acceptance_mark("API-JOURNAL", nrow(journal()) > 0,
                "journal present: $(nrow(journal())) records")
acceptance_mark("API-JOURNAL", nrow(journal_failures()) == 0,
                "all $(nrow(journal())) journal records passed")
print(acceptance_md(journal_origin_table()))
```
Table: The check journal by kind of claim

Two readings of that table matter for what this chapter establishes. The rows marked
`metida` are internal baselines: they fix behaviour and are not evidence of correctness, and
they are the largest single group. The `relation` rows carry no stored constant at all - the
expected value is computed in the same run from other quantities of the same model - and the
`property` rows carry none either. External evidence inside this chapter amounts to the
`spss` rows alone; the external case is made in the *Performance qualification* chapter,
which is why the two chapters are kept apart.

