# Performance qualification

The purpose of these procedures is to demonstrate performance on the critical tasks. The
goal is not to demonstrate every capability of the software, but to establish that the
analysis of specific narrow tasks is correct on wide data examples. Public datasets are used
as reference data, and the output is compared with results in publications and with results
from other statistical software: MixedModels.jl and IBM SPSS.

## Validation programme

Validation answers one question: do the numbers agree with an outside reference. This is
distinct from the operation qualification above, which checks that each public method
behaves as documented and whose references are in the main the package's own values. The
registry of both sets of procedures is in `test/REGISTRY.md`.

The programme has three categories, chosen by the kind of reference available and by the
kind of task:

| # | Procedure | Reference | Scope | Acceptance |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `validation_mm.jl` | MixedModels.jl (same run) and IBM SPSS | 11 models on 5 public datasets | `-2logREML` to 1e-6 against SPSS, 1e-4 against MixedModels.jl |
| 2 | `validation_be.jl` | IBM SPSS; Schütz et al. 2020 for the intervals | 30 bioequivalence datasets, models B and C | `-2logREML` to the SPSS value, and the 90 % confidence interval of the treatment contrast to the published table |
| 3 | `validation_spss.jl` | IBM SPSS | covariance-parameter inference, 8 models | every printed element of `W = 2H^{-1}`, standard errors, Type III, Satterthwaite DF |

### Rationale for the three categories

**Category 1** fixes the objective function and the point estimates on data that are public
and widely used, so a third party can reproduce them. Where the covariance structure can be
written in MixedModels.jl, both packages fit the same model in the same run and the values
are compared directly; there is no stored constant that can go stale, and the two
implementations share no code. Where the structure cannot be written in MixedModels.jl
(`CS`, `CSH`, `ARH`, `ARMA`, `AR`), the reference is IBM SPSS, quoted together with the
syntax that produced it.

**Category 2** covers the task the package is used for in practice. A bioequivalence
decision is made on a confidence interval, not on the objective function, so the interval is
part of the acceptance criteria: it is taken from `confint`, the path a user takes, and
compared with the published table (Schütz et al., AAPS J 2020;22(2):44, Table II, method B)
to half of its last digit. All sixty bounds agree exactly at that precision. Thirty
reference datasets are used rather than one, which exposes the convergence behaviour: eight
of them carry an SPSS warning about a non-positive-definite Hessian and six about
non-convergence, and those are listed in the procedure with wider tolerances.

**Category 3** goes one level deeper than the first two. Agreement on `-2logREML` and on the
point estimates does not establish that the standard errors and the degrees of freedom are
right, because those come from the asymptotic covariance of the covariance parameters,
`W = 2H^{-1}`. SPSS prints that matrix under `/PRINT=COVB`, which makes it checkable: 36
diagonal and 100 off-diagonal elements across 8 models, plus the Type III table row by row,
a parameter on the boundary of its space, and a rank-deficient model. Known disagreements
are recorded as defect markers rather than hidden behind loose tolerances; the evidence that
each belongs to the reference and not to Metida is in the header of that file.

